---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PanelHead from '../../components/PanelHead.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="PowerPoint Pen Toolbox — Andrew Shiau"
  description="Redesigning PowerPoint’s inking toolbox through 20+ working prototypes and a test that proved the team’s favourite design wrong."
>
  <!-- THE SPLIT (chooser round 2: concept 02, scope 03). Same `.pin` /
       `.splitrule` / `.feed` children at the same 4:8 columns as the index, so the
       site's one vertical division doesn't move when you click into a case study. -->
  <div class="grid">
    <PanelHead href="/work/powerpoint/" title="PowerPoint Pen Toolbox">
      <!-- The masthead: metadata you read before the story. On a Type A page the facts block
           is ALWAYS this, never a closing colophon (which is Type B's job) — and in the split
           it is IN the panel, because "before the story" is what a column that never leaves
           the screen means. -->
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Role</div>
        <div class="ftx">UX prototyper — <b>prototyping, usability testing, A/B testing, UX design</b>.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Tools</div>
        <div class="ftx">Framer Classic · JavaScript · Sketch.</div>
      </div>
    </PanelHead>

    <div class="feed">
      <!-- c1–12 of the FEED's twelve sub-tracks, not c1–8: the dek's own 32em cap is
           narrower than 8 of the feed's 700px, so c1–8 would set a column the text
           never reaches. No top margin — the dek is the top of the reading column. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">
          Redesigning the Pen Toolbox in PowerPoint’s Draw tab through twenty-odd working
          prototypes. The last test proved the team’s favourite design wrong.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A, beat 2: the clip is the lede. The final redesign, running, before any prose. -->
      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
        <figure class="demo hang">
          <video src="/media/pp-final.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline
            preload="metadata" aria-label="Silent screen recording: the final Pen Toolbox redesign proposal."></video>
          <figcaption>The final redesign proposal.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>


      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>The problem</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              Millions of people use the Draw tab to get an idea onto a slide with a pen, and
              its tools didn’t behave the way they expected. A usability study of the shipped
              tab — eight users recreating two diagrams, sorted by affinity diagramming into
              six themes — produced <b>52 recommendations</b>.
            </p>
            <p>
              I joined after that study and spent <b>13 weeks</b> on it: I built candidate
              toolboxes from its findings, tested them across 20-plus working prototypes, and
              handed engineering a prototype to build from.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="c1-12"><h2>What changed</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Remove “add pen”</div>
            <div class="ftx">People ink with one tool over and over, so the add-pen button mostly added clutter. We gave one of each tool and moved “add” to a long-press for the users who wanted more.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Eraser = a pen</div>
            <div class="ftx">Users reached for the eraser the way they reached for a pen, so we moved it into the toolbox and gave it its own width and type dropdown.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Dropdown refresh</div>
            <div class="ftx">The ink preview and width slider now show the real width and colour, “colours” and “effects” merged into one panel, and the recently-used-colours algorithm was retuned.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="block c1-6">
          <span class="st">“If we didn’t prototype, we’d have shipped one of the first three models as the spec.”</span>
          <!-- Named, per the annotation ("we can tag Adam Riddle, UX Design"). An anonymous
               "the UX designer on the team" reads as either invented or as a colleague being
               kept off the page; a name and a role is a real credit, and it is the one place on
               the site someone else's work is quoted. -->
          <span class="sd">— Adam Riddle, UX Design.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>Why a functional prototype</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              I built each design as a <b>working</b> prototype in Framer Classic: a real
              toolbox over an HTML canvas you could draw on, with an ink drawer I wrote that
              listens for mouse-move and lays down SVG. That let us test a prototype the same
              way we’d test the shipped product, by handing someone a drawing task.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- Four clips in TWO ALIGNED COLUMNS, cropped to one ratio (annotation: "this staggered
           thing no longer works, it should probably be rationalized as two columns, and aspect
           ratio cropped a bit on the taller videos to match").
           This was a diagonal scatter, and the scatter existed to hide a defect in the assets:
           two of these recordings are 999x562 and the rest are 1000x626, so a two-up row put a
           taller frame beside a shorter one and the taller read as bottom-cropped. Making sure no
           two frames ever shared a baseline hid that — at the cost of the loudest composition on
           a site whose structure is otherwise whitespace on one column.
           `.mscatter` now crops both shapes to 1000/626 with object-fit, so the mismatch is
           fixed rather than concealed and two columns just work. See global.css. -->
      <div class="matrix mscatter">
        <figure class="demo">
          <video src="/media/pp-prototypeInUse.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline
            preload="metadata" aria-label="Silent screen recording: drawing on the working prototype with a pen."></video>
          <figcaption>The prototype in use. You draw on it with a real pen.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="demo">
          <video src="/media/pp-arrow.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline
            preload="metadata" aria-label="Silent screen recording: an arrow marking which colours can be modified."></video>
          <figcaption>An arrow marks which colours can be modified — a fix testing found.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="demo">
          <video src="/media/pp-colorJump.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline
            preload="metadata" aria-label="Silent screen recording: a colour jumping between slots, with an enlarge animation."></video>
          <figcaption>A colour that jumped between slots got an enlarge animation.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="demo">
          <video src="/media/pp-longPress.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline
            preload="metadata" aria-label="Silent screen recording: a long press adding a pen to the toolbox."></video>
          <figcaption>Long-press adds a pen — the power-user path that replaced the button.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A, the alternatives beat (round-3 sheet, Q05): the three candidates, before the
           result. This page's alternatives are the best-sourced on the site — three built
           designs and an A/B between two of them — and they used to be compressed into the
           first paragraph of the closing section, where the outcome overshadowed them. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>The three we built</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              The team wanted colour and thickness at the top level of the UI and was
              confident about it. So we built three ways to pick a colour rather than argue
              about them. Two of the three mattered: a <b>ribbon</b> that put the swatches in
              the top-level toolbox, and a plain <b>dropdown</b>. Each candidate went in front
              of users, and what tripped them got fixed — four of those fixes are in the clips
              above.
            </p>
            <p>
              The ribbon was the favourite for most of the project. It cost the most
              engineering, so it was the one that had to be right, which is why it’s the one
              we tested against a control instead of shipping on confidence.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A close: a named final section. This was the page's most important claim and it
           used to sit in an unheaded prose block, which read as an afterthought. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>Outcome</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              We <b>A/B-tested</b> the ribbon against the plain dropdown, holding everything
              else constant. The ribbon lost: its benefit was too small to pay for the
              engineering, and the plain dropdown shipped. The final proposal took the add-pen
              button out and moved the eraser into the toolbox as one more pen, so the toolbox
              holds one of each tool.
            </p>
            <p>
              We had been confident about that ribbon for most of the project and we were
              wrong. We found out because the prototype was real enough to hand someone a
              drawing task.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
